WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:16.420
Your doctor may recommend
a craniotomy or craniectomy

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.090
procedure to treat a
number of different brain

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:23.200
diseases, injuries,
or conditions.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.840
Your skull is made
of bone, and serves

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.500
as a hard, protective
covering for your brain.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:34.080
Just inside your skull,
three layers of tissue

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.640
called meninges
surround your brain.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.410
The thick, outermost
layer is the dura mater.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:48.660
The middle tissue layer
is the arachnoid mater,

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:52.990
and the innermost
layer is the pia mater.

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:55.660
Between the arachnoid
mater and the pia mater

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:58.830
is the subarachnoid
space, which contains

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:05.070
blood vessels and a clear fluid
called cerebrospinal fluid.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.540
Blood vessels, called
bridging veins,

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:13.250
connect the surface of your
brain with the dura mater.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.970
Other blood vessels,
called cerebral arteries,

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:18.970
bring blood to your brain.

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:21.620
Inside your skull,
normal brain function

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:24.110
requires a delicate
balance of pressure

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:26.810
between the blood in
your blood vessels,

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:30.400
the cerebrospinal fluid
that surrounds your brain,

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.050
and your brain tissue.

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:37.700
This is called normal
intracranial pressure.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.530
Increased intracranial
pressure may

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:46.510
result from brain
tumors, head injuries,

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:49.550
problems with your
blood vessels,

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:55.000
or infections in your
brain or spinal cord.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:57.920
These conditions put
pressure on your brain,

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:00.100
and may cause it
to swell or change

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:10.900
shape inside your skull, which
can lead to serious brain injury.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.210
Your doctor may
recommend a craniotomy

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:17.620
to remove abnormal brain
tissue such as a brain

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:24.500
tumor, a sample of
tissue by biopsy,

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:32.740
a blood clot called a hematoma,
excess cerebrospinal fluid,

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:37.400
or pus from an infection
called an abscess.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:44.770
A craniotomy may also be done
to relieve brain swelling;

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:48.830
stop bleeding,
called a hemorrhage;

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:56.330
repair abnormal blood vessels;
repair skull fractures;

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:59.800
or repair damaged meninges.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.020
Finally, a craniotomy
may also be

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:10.370
done to treat brain
conditions such as epilepsy,

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:14.160
deliver medication
to your brain,

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:20.700
or implant a medical device
such as a deep brain stimulator.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:23.750
The most common reason
for a craniotomy

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:27.800
is to remove a brain tumor.

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:31.680
Before your procedure, you will
be given general anesthesia

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:35.930
to make you unconscious
and pain-free.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:37.950
The skin on your
scalp will be shaved

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:42.500
over the location of the tumor.

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:48.490
To begin, your surgeon will
make an incision in your scalp.

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:53.340
One or more small holes
will be made in your skull.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.490
Then, your surgeon
will connect the holes

00:03:55.490 --> 00:04:00.210
to create a circular piece
of bone called a bone flap,

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:03.460
and remove it.

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:06.660
Your surgeon will make an
incision in the dura mater

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:09.690
to expose your brain.

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:13.770
Then your tumor will be removed.

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:19.050
The incision in the dura mater
will be closed with sutures.

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:23.800
Your surgeon will reattach the
bone flap with metal plates

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:29.600
and screws that will remain
in your skull for life.

00:04:30.629 --> 00:04:33.590
You may have a variation
on a craniotomy called

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:36.920
a craniectomy, if your
brain is very swollen

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:40.020
or your skull is infected.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.790
Your surgeon may delay
reattaching the bone flap

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:45.880
until a later procedure, to
allow time for the swelling

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:48.600
to go down.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:53.040
At the end of either a
craniotomy or a craniectomy,

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.530
your scalp incision will
be closed with staples,

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:02.600
and your head will be
wrapped in bandages.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:05.710
If you had a craniectomy, you
will have another procedure

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:08.430
called a cranioplasty
at a later date,

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:12.600
after the swelling goes down.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:15.810
At that time, your surgeon
will reattach your bone flap

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:20.900
with metal plates
and screws, then

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:25.800
close the skin
incision with staples.

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:28.730
After either procedure,
you will stay

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:32.670
in the hospital
for several days.

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:34.990
Your caregiver will
check your brain function

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:40.710
by asking you questions, and
shining a light in your eyes.

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:44.660
You may be given medication
to prevent complications.

